# `s/\(ab\)c/YES/` -- a group, which this does not implement.
#
# **This was silently wrong until 2026-09-01 and the header said it was not.**
# `pattern.sol` read `\(` as a literal parenthesis, so a valid sed script came
# out inverted on both counts: the line containing the literal text `(ab)c` was
# substituted and the line containing `abc` was not. No error, no exit status.
# The sed.sol header had claimed for the life of the file that such a script
# "will be refused rather than misread", which was the behaviour nobody had run.
#
# Sixty cases in agree/ and not one of them used `\(`, which is why the harness
# this repository trusts most never saw it. An author-written corpus tests what
# its author thought of; that is the whole argument for holding a program
# against an oracle, and it applies to the corpus as well.
#
# It is refused now, by lib/pattern.sol rather than here, because the library is
# what knows its own subset. Real sed substitutes on the second line; this exits
# 1 and says which construct it does not have. Both answers are honest and they
# are not the same bytes, which is why this case lives in differ/.
args: 's/\(ab\)c/YES/'
input:
xx (ab)c xx
yy abc yy
zz nothing zz
